/**
 * @page topic_quic QUIC and WebTransport
 * @ingroup module_network
 * @brief Native QUIC and browser WebTransport on the shared ACIP transport layer.
 *
 * QUIC is an optional UDP transport for ACIP. It is selected explicitly with a
 * `quic://` endpoint on native clients; it does not replace TCP, WebSocket, or
 * WebRTC. Browser clients reach the same listener through HTTP/3 WebTransport.
 * Both paths still run the normal ACIP handshake and application authentication.
 *
 * @section quic_endpoints Endpoints and configuration
 *
 * A native server enables QUIC with a concrete bind address, UDP port, TLS
 * certificate, and private key:
 * @code{.sh}
 * ascii-chat server --quic-bind-address 0.0.0.0 --quic-port 27224 \
 *   --quic-tls-cert server.crt --quic-tls-key server.key
 * @endcode
 *
 * A native client connects explicitly and may provide a private CA:
 * @code{.sh}
 * ascii-chat client quic://chat.example:27224 --quic-ca-file CA.pem
 * @endcode
 *
 * `--quic-port 0` uses the TCP server port for the listener. TCP remains
 * available. A certificate must match the hostname or IP used by the client;
 * clients use the system trust store unless `--quic-ca-file` is supplied.
 *
 * @section quic_layers Transport and security
 *
 * `include/ascii-chat/network/acip/quic.h` owns the endpoint and transport
 * lifecycle. The resulting `acip_transport_t` is consumed by the same
 * connection, negotiation, ACIP dispatch, and outbound scheduler used by other
 * backends. Callers must not create a second QUIC receive loop or bypass the
 * transport abstraction.
 *
 * QUIC TLS requires TLS 1.3 and authenticates the transport endpoint. It does
 * not replace ACIP authentication, identity pinning, or the encrypted-record
 * policy. The transport is not ready for media until TLS and the ACIP security
 * handshake have both completed; `quic_transport_allow_media()` enforces that
 * barrier.
 *
 * @section quic_delivery Streams and datagrams
 *
 * Reliable ACIP traffic is split into independent control, audio, and visual
 * streams. Each stream begins with an `ACQS` class preamble, followed by the
 * existing ACIP packet headers and payloads. Packet length and CRC remain ACIP
 * responsibilities. Ordering is guaranteed within a stream, not across all
 * streams, so control transitions are acknowledged before dependent media is
 * admitted.
 *
 * QUIC DATAGRAM is optional and negotiated. Audio and visual datagrams are
 * preferences, not guarantees: if the peer or path does not support them, the
 * scheduler uses reliable streams. A datagram that expires or is rejected for
 * congestion is not silently retried as reliable media. Fragmented media uses
 * bounded reassembly and can request a keyframe after loss.
 *
 * @section quic_flow_control Backpressure and ownership
 *
 * Reliable sends are retained until ngtcp2 acknowledges their bytes and count
 * against a bounded per-connection queue. The shared scheduler prioritizes
 * control, then audio, then visual traffic, and returns an explicit error when
 * admission is impossible. Receive callbacks borrow packet data only until the
 * callback returns; consumers that retain it must copy it.
 *
 * Stop producers, join transport users, interrupt pending I/O, and then release
 * the transport and endpoint. A failed join leaves live dependencies intact so
 * shutdown can be retried safely.
 *
 * @section quic_webtransport WebTransport
 *
 * The optional HTTP/3 listener shares the QUIC UDP endpoint. ALPN selects raw
 * `acip/1` or `h3`; WebTransport validates the configured HTTPS origin and
 * `/acip` path before mapping streams and HTTP datagrams into ACIP flows. ACDS
 * may advertise a native QUIC port and a public WebTransport URL independently.
 * ACDS signaling remains on its existing authenticated connection; peer media
 * uses the independently established QUIC/WebTransport ACIP session.
 *
 * @section quic_migration Migration and fallback
 *
 * The native endpoint can migrate a confirmed connection to another local
 * address in the same address family. Producers must be paused during the
 * operation; TLS, ACIP keys, stream positions, and replay state remain attached
 * to the connection. A failed bind preserves the old socket.
 *
 * Native fallback to TCP is opt-in with `--quic-fallback-port` and requires
 * ACIP encryption plus the pinned `--server-key`. Certificate, authentication,
 * protocol, and cancellation failures do not trigger fallback, and an explicit
 * QUIC URL does not silently switch transports.
 *
 * @section quic_testing Testing and limitations
 *
 * The detailed protocol record and browser test commands remain in
 * @ref md_docs_2quic.html. QUIC tests cover native streams, negotiated
 * datagrams, TLS/identity failures, discovery advertisements, WebTransport,
 * and path migration. The private UDP driver is experimental and must not be
 * exposed directly to untrusted networks; production admission and the shared
 * connection lifecycle remain the supported integration path.
 *
 * @see topic_network
 * @see md_docs_2transport-backpressure.html
 * @see md_docs_2quic.html
 */
